00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:28.110
So Stefani, hello.

00:00:28.110 --> 00:00:28.650
Good morning.

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:29.490
At least in my time.

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:30.779
Hi.

00:00:30.779 --> 00:00:31.560
Hi, Steve.

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:33.600
It's great to be here on your channel.

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:39.600
Could you, Stefani, give us a
brief introduction to you and,

00:00:39.605 --> 00:00:43.320
and your activities as, as it
concerns learning languages?

00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:44.129
Yeah.

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:48.989
I have been into learning languages for
a very long time and I actually grew up

00:00:48.989 --> 00:00:53.160
in a monolingual household surrounded
by a lot of dialects, uh, which is

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:59.985
normal in Bulgaria, but truly just one
language at home and not really much

00:00:59.985 --> 00:01:01.964
that I would hear outside of my home.

00:01:02.295 --> 00:01:06.855
So for me it was a journey that
was about the love of languages.

00:01:06.855 --> 00:01:11.505
And I liked it even at school where, you
know, I often argue that it's not the best

00:01:11.565 --> 00:01:15.195
way to learn the way that is presented
at school, but I enjoyed it even then.

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:18.945
And I would always do this outside
activities, you know, outside of

00:01:18.945 --> 00:01:22.815
school and you know, a lot of getting
a lot of input into language learning.

00:01:23.235 --> 00:01:27.195
And for me it was really Spanish
that opened the floodgates

00:01:27.195 --> 00:01:30.735
because with Spanish, which was
not available at my school at the

00:01:30.735 --> 00:01:32.354
time, and I really wanted to learn.

00:01:32.744 --> 00:01:35.235
And with that language I
figured out, you know, how to

00:01:35.235 --> 00:01:36.914
learn by myself and everything.

00:01:36.945 --> 00:01:41.341
And from then on I'd added more and
more languages as I find it fascinating.

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:47.550
And, um, you know, a number of things
that you said there are quite interesting.

00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:50.160
One is that you grew up in
a monolingual family, right?

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:54.179
Uh, and I remember, I remember I was
at a Polyglot Conference in Montreal

00:01:54.179 --> 00:01:58.645
and talking to 600 people and I said,
how many people here grew up in a mo...

00:01:58.975 --> 00:02:01.830
grew up in a family that had more
than one language, and practically

00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:02.970
no one put their hand up.

00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:06.620
So the myth that you have to
be raised in a multilingual

00:02:06.620 --> 00:02:08.280
family it's simply not true.

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:11.100
It can be a positive factor, but
it's certainly not necessary.

00:02:11.490 --> 00:02:11.980
Yeah.

00:02:11.985 --> 00:02:16.050
Uh, the second thing is Bulgaria,
you say a lot of different dialects.

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:16.170
Mm-hmm.

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:18.600
How different are these
dialects from each other?

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:22.290
Are they still Bulgarian or are they
different sort of slavi languages?

00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:26.160
Right, so they are, they're still
Bulgarian, or at least that's how

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:27.780
they're classified by linguists.

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:33.420
If, if somebody from the eastern part
of the country, like closer to the

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:37.650
Black Sea or something goes to the
Southwest, for example, and like,

00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:42.240
especially in villages where the dialects
are very strong, chances are they're

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:44.050
not gonna be able to understand much.

00:02:44.390 --> 00:02:51.480
So, I really, it, it's, I do think it's,
uh, the same language, but you know,

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:53.370
sometimes we don't understand each other.

00:02:53.370 --> 00:02:55.170
If we are speaking in.

00:02:56.760 --> 00:02:57.250
Yeah.

00:02:57.510 --> 00:03:02.130
So, and, and of course Macedonian of
course is a separate language, right.

00:03:02.610 --> 00:03:05.310
But is has some similarity with Bulgarian.

00:03:05.310 --> 00:03:09.240
Is there sort of a gradual transition
from Bulgarian to Macedonian and

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:11.760
then from Macedonian to say Serbian?

00:03:11.765 --> 00:03:17.310
Is there a, a, a gradual, uh, progression
there, or are they all distinct?

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:25.575
Yeah, so with Macedonian, There's a whole
lot of political issue there, um, mm-hmm.

00:03:25.815 --> 00:03:30.135
. So I'm gonna try to kind of skate
around that as, as well as I can and

00:03:30.195 --> 00:03:32.415
not really get too involved into that.

00:03:32.420 --> 00:03:35.834
But, um, generally linguists disagree.

00:03:35.894 --> 00:03:40.170
Um, there's quite a lot of, you
know, some say that, many actually

00:03:40.170 --> 00:03:42.870
say that it is really the same
language, kind of part of a dialect

00:03:42.870 --> 00:03:46.800
continuum, the way that Norwegian
and Swedish would be in Danish maybe.

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:50.010
Um mm-hmm . So it's kind of
a dialect continuum between

00:03:50.010 --> 00:03:51.450
Macedonian and Bulgarian.

00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:53.040
And most linguists say that.

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:57.570
And of course there's a lot of like other
views which are mainly driven by politics.

00:03:57.570 --> 00:03:58.980
And again, I don't wanna touch that.

00:03:59.579 --> 00:04:03.630
Um, but really depends where you are from.

00:04:03.630 --> 00:04:06.210
I would say like what part
of Bulgaria you are from.

00:04:06.945 --> 00:04:07.655
Um, mm-hmm.

00:04:07.740 --> 00:04:11.415
for example, there is actually a region
in Bulgaria that's called Macedonia.

00:04:11.955 --> 00:04:15.675
Um, as well as in Greece of
course, but in Greece they mostly

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:19.365
speak Greek and the region of
Bulgaria that's called Macedonia.

00:04:19.365 --> 00:04:23.745
And what in the Republic of
Macedonia is spoken uh, there's like

00:04:23.745 --> 00:04:25.545
quite a lot of similarity there.

00:04:25.550 --> 00:04:30.825
Obviously in, in towns, cities it is
not so much, but in villages it's like

00:04:30.855 --> 00:04:31.995
they speak almost, almost the same.

00:04:33.185 --> 00:04:37.505
Um, but if you look at Northeast
Bulgaria, they wouldn't be able to

00:04:37.505 --> 00:04:39.185
understand Macedonian very well.

00:04:39.525 --> 00:04:42.075
Just because they're much
further away from the border.

00:04:42.344 --> 00:04:48.675
So it's kind of, yeah, the dialects
really play into question there.

00:04:48.675 --> 00:04:52.815
But, and with Serbian, cause
you mentioned Serbian as well.

00:04:52.935 --> 00:04:57.855
I would say that Macedonian as
in like what is spoken in, um,

00:04:57.915 --> 00:05:01.155
the country of Macedonia perhaps
borrows a lot from Serbian.

00:05:01.295 --> 00:05:02.025
I, I would say.

00:05:02.025 --> 00:05:02.815
So there's...

00:05:03.685 --> 00:05:07.185
that interesting uh, kind
of dynamic that's happening.

00:05:07.925 --> 00:05:09.335
Yeah, it's, mm-hmm.

00:05:09.414 --> 00:05:11.215
, certainly quite a lot of exchange.

00:05:11.215 --> 00:05:12.914
So, yes.

00:05:12.914 --> 00:05:17.685
And you know, it's interesting is
in French, the mixed vegetable dish

00:05:17.685 --> 00:05:19.515
is called ... did you know that?

00:05:19.685 --> 00:05:20.175
Yeah.

00:05:20.180 --> 00:05:20.445
Yeah.

00:05:20.474 --> 00:05:22.935
This refers to the fact that
there's such a variety of

00:05:22.935 --> 00:05:24.495
languages spoken in the Balkans.

00:05:24.495 --> 00:05:24.945
I guess.

00:05:25.155 --> 00:05:25.645
I don't know.

00:05:25.995 --> 00:05:29.474
So from a background of all these
different languages and dialects,

00:05:29.474 --> 00:05:32.295
you go out and learn other languages.

00:05:32.625 --> 00:05:36.135
Can you tell us how many languages you
speak and what was your experience on your

00:05:36.135 --> 00:05:37.875
path towards learning these languages?

00:05:38.775 --> 00:05:42.285
Yeah, so for me, I, I always
distinguish between two things, like

00:05:42.290 --> 00:05:44.594
how many I speak and how many I learn.

00:05:45.205 --> 00:05:45.795
Um mm-hmm.

00:05:45.880 --> 00:05:46.425
and

00:05:46.430 --> 00:05:48.225
good, good advice.

00:05:48.435 --> 00:05:48.795
Yes.

00:05:49.515 --> 00:05:49.875
Yeah.

00:05:49.965 --> 00:05:53.235
I usually, I say currently
that I speak eight.

00:05:53.285 --> 00:05:58.425
And for me, it's speak kind of, I kind
of feel comfortable to say I speak a

00:05:58.425 --> 00:06:00.435
language at around the intermediate level.

00:06:00.855 --> 00:06:03.465
Uh, when I'm a beginner,
I don't feel comfortable.

00:06:04.034 --> 00:06:08.594
So yeah, that's, I would say that
out of those I would, I'm fluent in

00:06:08.594 --> 00:06:13.305
five and intermediate in three, and
I study a couple of more currently

00:06:13.310 --> 00:06:16.575
Persian and Russian, but I don't
say I speak them because they're

00:06:16.635 --> 00:06:18.315
just in their beginning stages.

00:06:19.185 --> 00:06:22.515
Although Russian is weird 'cause I,
I've always been able to understand

00:06:22.515 --> 00:06:28.280
it just by virtue of being Slavic,
but not really speak it, so yeah.

00:06:28.280 --> 00:06:29.580
It's, it's always been...

00:06:29.580 --> 00:06:30.480
which are the five?

00:06:30.570 --> 00:06:31.470
Which are the five?

00:06:32.310 --> 00:06:33.070
The, the, yeah.

00:06:33.070 --> 00:06:34.040
Where you're comfortable.

00:06:34.470 --> 00:06:34.890
Yeah.

00:06:34.950 --> 00:06:39.450
So obviously my native one
and English, and um, then

00:06:39.480 --> 00:06:42.000
Spanish, Portuguese and Italian.

00:06:42.330 --> 00:06:42.510
Yeah.

00:06:42.515 --> 00:06:42.940
Mm-hmm.

00:06:43.305 --> 00:06:45.150
very focused on the romance group,

00:06:46.260 --> 00:06:47.820
and the three intermediate languages?

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:49.760
French, German, and Greek.

00:06:50.660 --> 00:06:51.310
Aha.

00:06:51.550 --> 00:06:52.290
Greek.

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:53.159
Very good.

00:06:53.310 --> 00:06:53.580
Yeah.

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:59.969
Uh, first let me tell you that your use
of English to me is, is a model of how

00:06:59.969 --> 00:07:01.984
people should speak in another language.

00:07:02.385 --> 00:07:02.895
No one.

00:07:02.895 --> 00:07:04.560
Would you think that you
are a native speaker.

00:07:05.130 --> 00:07:10.110
Uh, because there's a trace of an
unidentifiable something accent that

00:07:10.110 --> 00:07:11.370
says you're not a native speaker.

00:07:11.860 --> 00:07:16.500
However, your use of phrasing, your use
of words is a hundred percent native.

00:07:16.950 --> 00:07:21.750
Not only native, but at a higher
level than many, many natives.

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:26.180
And I've always said, you know,
people strive to be, you know,

00:07:26.420 --> 00:07:27.600
mistaken for a native, forget it.

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:28.669
You're not gonna achieve that.

00:07:29.169 --> 00:07:34.845
Focus instead on, on using words,
you know, in, in a, in a economical,

00:07:34.850 --> 00:07:37.545
intelligent, accurate, uh, elegant way.

00:07:37.725 --> 00:07:38.285
Mm-hmm.

00:07:38.365 --> 00:07:41.265
And if you do those things and you
pull it off with a slight accent,

00:07:41.475 --> 00:07:45.900
in fact, it sounds better than
if you try to not have an accent,

00:07:45.900 --> 00:07:48.990
but you can't use words properly,
I think that should be the model.

00:07:49.290 --> 00:07:51.870
When we learn a foreign language, you
speak English the way you speak English.

00:07:51.870 --> 00:07:52.650
Very impressed.

00:07:52.770 --> 00:07:53.460
Very impressed.

00:07:53.550 --> 00:07:54.030
Thank you.

00:07:54.360 --> 00:07:56.130
Thanks so much for the kind words.

00:07:56.130 --> 00:08:00.360
And actually, just to add, uh,
something quick here, I, I've never

00:08:00.365 --> 00:08:04.800
been concerned with accents, which,
um, yeah, it's just never bothered me.

00:08:04.950 --> 00:08:10.710
And also I think it really depends
kind of how your mouth moves.

00:08:10.710 --> 00:08:13.530
It's really weird, but you know, when
your, when your mouth is moving a

00:08:13.530 --> 00:08:17.220
certain way, thanks to your native
language, and sometimes it picks up

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:19.320
easier, some accents than others.

00:08:19.325 --> 00:08:23.730
And I've noticed right from day one,
I had a really good accent in Greek,

00:08:24.090 --> 00:08:28.080
better than my English accent, but my
Greek is much worse than my English.

00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:30.000
So there, there's that.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:34.539
I, I would think too, I, I think
particularly the vowels I would imagine.

00:08:34.610 --> 00:08:38.820
Like Greek has very standard vowel
sounds like Spanish, Greek, and

00:08:38.820 --> 00:08:43.620
I presume Bulgarian and English,
uh, like, uh, Swedish, uh, there's

00:08:43.870 --> 00:08:45.870
a lot of dip thong Portuguese.

00:08:45.900 --> 00:08:45.990
Mm-hmm.

00:08:46.435 --> 00:08:49.140
dip thong type sounds
that are more different.

00:08:49.155 --> 00:08:51.735
So it's gonna be more
difficult to to, to hit those.

00:08:52.245 --> 00:08:53.445
Um, where...

00:08:53.445 --> 00:08:56.395
did you study abroad or how did you
go about learning these languages?

00:08:57.314 --> 00:08:57.584
. 
Yeah.

00:08:57.645 --> 00:09:01.665
So I did, uh, study abroad in, in the US.

00:09:01.755 --> 00:09:05.444
I, I did a year in the US
when I was, um, 11th grade.

00:09:05.505 --> 00:09:05.594
Mm-hmm.

00:09:06.175 --> 00:09:09.974
And then I also, I went to an
American university here in Bulgaria.

00:09:09.980 --> 00:09:12.824
And then after that, you know,
where the American university

00:09:12.824 --> 00:09:14.655
